---
import DocsLayout from "@layouts/docs/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Containers vs Islands" currentPath="/docs/containers">
  <section class="docs-hero">
    <span class="docs-label">Architecture</span>
    <h1>Containers vs. Islands</h1>
    <p class="docs-intro">
      While Astro adopts an islands architecture with other frameworks,
      Qwik uses a different strategy known as Qwik containers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <h2>How containers work</h2>
    <p>
      Despite the differences in approach, both share similar
      limitations. In the DOM, notice there aren't any
      <code>&lt;astro-island&gt;</code> custom elements — this is
      because to Astro, Qwik looks like static data.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      In Qwik, the handlers themselves are the roots / entrypoints
      of the application.
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <h2>Communicating across containers</h2>
    <p>
      One common limitation is trying to pass state into another
      island or container. Sharing state is crucial in modern web
      development.
    </p>

    <h3>Why not use global signals or nanostores?</h3>
    <p>
      Other frameworks with Astro address this by using
      <a href="https://github.com/nanostores/nanostores">nano stores</a>,
      or global signals. We do not recommend this approach — they can
      lead to unexpected behavior in an SSR context.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      On the server, global state is shared across requests, and the
      lack of data isolation can (and will) lead to bugs, memory leaks
      and has security implications.
    </blockquote>

    <h3>Custom Events</h3>
    <p>
      In Qwik, it was a design decision to not include global signal
      state. Instead, use <strong>custom events</strong>, which offer
      several advantages:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Performance (avoid unnecessary state synchronization)</li>
      <li>Does not wake up the framework on page load</li>
      <li>Micro Frontend (MFE) Support</li>
      <li>Different versions can exist on the page</li>
      <li>Event Driven</li>
      <li>Decoupled</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <a href="https://github.com/thejackshelton/astro-qwik-global-state-example/blob/main/src/components/counter.tsx">This example</a>
      shows how custom events can be used throughout your application.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <h2>Named Slots</h2>
    <p>
      For named slots within Astro, instead of adding
      <code>q:slot</code> on the markup, add <code>slot</code>
      instead.
    </p>
    <div class="code-block">
      <div class="code-header">
        <span class="code-filename">my-slot-comp.tsx</span>
      </div>
      <div class="code-body">
        <Code
          code={`import { Slot, component$ } from "@qwik.dev/core";

export const MySlotComp = component$(() => {
  return (
    <>
      <Slot name="test" />
    </>
  );
});`}
          lang="tsx"
          theme="github-dark"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="code-block">
      <div class="code-header">
        <span class="code-filename">index.astro</span>
      </div>
      <div class="code-body">
        <Code
          code={`<MySlotComp>
  <div slot="test">Content inside the slot named test!</div>
</MySlotComp>`}
          lang="astro"
          theme="github-dark"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .docs-hero {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-xl);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--neutral-950);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .docs-label {
    font-size: 10px;
    color: var(--rose-500);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  h1 { font-size: var(--step-5); margin-bottom: var(--space-m); }

  .docs-intro {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--neutral-400);
    max-width: 48ch;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .docs-section {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-xl);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--neutral-950);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .docs-section:last-child { border-bottom: none; }

  h2 { font-size: var(--step-2); margin-bottom: var(--space-m); }
  h3 { font-size: var(--step-1); margin-bottom: var(--space-s); }

  p {
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    color: var(--neutral-400);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-m);
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  p code {
    color: var(--green-400);
    font-family: monospace;
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }

  a { color: var(--purple-300); text-underline-offset: 4px; }
  strong { color: var(--purple-50); }

  blockquote {
    border-left: var(--space-4xs) dotted var(--rose-500);
    padding-left: var(--space-m);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-m);
    color: var(--neutral-400);
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    line-height: 1.7;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  ul {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-m);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-3xs);
  }

  li {
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    color: var(--neutral-400);
    padding-left: var(--space-m);
    position: relative;
  }

  li::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.7em;
    width: 6px;
    height: 6px;
    background-color: var(--rose-500);
  }

  .code-block {
    border: 1px solid var(--neutral-950);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-m);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .code-header {
    background-color: #1a1a1a;
    padding: var(--space-3xs) var(--space-m);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--neutral-950);
  }

  .code-filename {
    font-size: 10px;
    color: var(--neutral-400);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .code-body {
    padding: var(--space-m);
    background-color: #151515;
  }

  .code-body :global(pre) {
    background: transparent !important;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }
</style>
